    "reply": "The retrieved variant record for rs334 carries mixed clinical significance assertions [1]. The literature matches include one HGVS-style name alongside several distinct rsIDs [2][3]. None of the findings names an associated condition, so the disease link cannot be stated from these results.\n\n## Clinical significance\n\nThe variant record lists clinical_significance: not-provided, protective, likely-benign, pathogenic, other [1]. This range suggests conflicting submitter interpretations rather than a single consensus call [1].\n\n## Literature variant names\n\nLitVar2 returned the variant name c.20A>T [2]. This is a coding-level HGVS description consistent with a single-nucleotide substitution near the start of a coding sequence [2]. The same search also returned rs334348 [3]. It returned rs334353 [4]. It returned c.-50T>C, a 5\u2032 untranslated region position [5]. It returned rs334773 [6]. These rsIDs are separate dbSNP entries that likely matched rs334 as a text prefix, so they should not be treated as synonyms of it [3][4][6].\n\n## Evidence gaps\n\nThe findings do not name the gene carrying rs334 or any associated disease; they give only the significance categories [1]. Confirming the condition would require a direct ClinVar or dbSNP record retrieval.",
